"""Authored Physics 11 lessons, adapted from the supplied textbook at algebra level."""
UNITS = []

def unit(number, title, summary, chapters, inquiry):
    u = dict(number=number, title=title, summary=summary, chapters=chapters,
             inquiry=inquiry, lessons=[])
    UNITS.append(u)
    return u

def lesson(u, title, refs, goal, explanation, equations, example, steps, pitfalls, practice):
    u['lessons'].append(dict(title=title, refs=refs, goal=goal, explanation=explanation,
        equations=equations, example=example, steps=steps, pitfalls=pitfalls, practice=practice))

u = unit(1, 'Math Tools & Measurement',
 'Build the measurement, algebra, graphing, and trigonometry habits that every later unit uses.',
 'Ch. 1; Ch. 2 §2-1 to §2-3; Ch. 3 §3-1',
 'Measure a small object five times with a ruler. State the ruler resolution, calculate the mean and spread, and distinguish repeated-reading variation from a possible zero error. Then time several walks over the same marked distance. Plot position against time, draw a best-fit line, and explain the slope with units. Change one method to reduce uncertainty and compare the evidence.')
lesson(u, 'SI units and dimensional reasoning', [('1-1',37),('1-2',41),('1-3',42)],
 'Convert a measurement to SI units and check whether an equation has consistent dimensions.',
 'A physical quantity combines a numerical value with a unit. A length of 2.0 m and a time of 2.0 s have different meanings even though the numbers match. In this course the main base units are metre (m), kilogram (kg), second (s), ampere (A), and kelvin (K). Derived units combine them: velocity uses m/s, acceleration uses m/s², and force uses N = kg·m/s².\n\nA conversion factor is a ratio of equal physical amounts, so it equals one. Put the unit you want to cancel on the opposite side of the fraction. For an area or volume, raise the entire length conversion to the appropriate power. Dimensional checks can reject an equation with incompatible units, but cannot determine a missing numerical factor or prove that the physics is correct.',
 'v = Δx/Δt  [m/s]\na = Δv/Δt  [m/s²]\n1 N = 1 kg·m/s²\n1 J = 1 N·m\n1 W = 1 J/s',
 'Convert 72.0 km/h to m/s and 250 cm² to m².',
 ['Write 72.0 km/h × (1000 m/1 km) × (1 h/3600 s). Both unwanted units cancel.',
  'The speed is 20.0 m/s. A speed equation that adds metres to seconds would fail a dimensional check.',
  'For area, 250 cm² × (1 m/100 cm)² = 0.0250 m² if 250 has three significant figures. The squared factor is 1/10 000, not 1/100.'],
 'Use kilograms rather than grams in mechanical equations. Keep prefixes case-sensitive: m means milli while M means mega. Never convert a squared unit using only a first-power length factor.',
 [('Convert 3.50 ms to seconds.', 'Milli means 10⁻³. The time is 3.50 × 10⁻³ s.'),
  ('Does Δx = v₀t + at have consistent units?', 'No. v₀t has units m, while at has units m/s. The constant-acceleration displacement term is ½at², with units m.')])
lesson(u, 'Scientific notation and significant figures', [('1-1',37)],
 'Report a calculation to a precision justified by its measurements.',
 'Scientific notation writes a number as a × 10ⁿ, with 1 ≤ |a| < 10. It makes very large and very small quantities manageable and makes the stated precision visible. Leading zeros locate the decimal point; they are not significant. Zeros between nonzero digits are significant. Trailing zeros after a decimal point are significant. A value written simply as 1200 has ambiguous precision; 1.20 × 10³ is unambiguous.\n\nFor multiplication and division, round the final result to the fewest significant figures among the measured inputs. For addition and subtraction, round to the least precise decimal place. Exact counts and defined conversion factors do not limit precision. Keep extra digits through intermediate steps, then round once at the end. These rules are a reporting convention, not a substitute for a full uncertainty analysis.',
 '(a × 10ᵐ)(b × 10ⁿ) = ab × 10ᵐ⁺ⁿ\n(a × 10ᵐ)/(b × 10ⁿ) = (a/b) × 10ᵐ⁻ⁿ',
 'A runner travels 12.40 m in 3.2 s. Report the average speed. Then add 12.40 m and 0.6 m.',
 ['The calculator gives 12.40/3.2 = 3.875 m/s.',
  'The time has two significant figures, so report the speed as 3.9 m/s.',
  'The sum is 13.00 m before rounding. The 0.6 m measurement is precise only to tenths, so the reported sum is 13.0 m.'],
 'Do not round every intermediate line. Do not report a long calculator display as though the experiment measured all those digits. Written zeros matter: 3.0 has two significant figures.',
 [('How many significant figures are in 0.004050?', 'Four: 4, the internal 0, 5, and the final decimal 0.'),
  ('Calculate (2.50 × 10³)(4.0 × 10⁻²).', 'The unrounded product is 100. The reported result is 1.0 × 10², with two significant figures.')])
lesson(u, 'Accuracy, precision, and uncertainty', [('1-1',37),('1-2',41)],
 'Describe the quality of data and judge whether an experimental result agrees with a model.',
 'Accuracy concerns closeness to a suitable reference value. Precision concerns the spread of repeated measurements. Repeated readings can cluster tightly while all being shifted by a calibration error. Random effects vary between trials; systematic effects shift results in a consistent way. Repeating measurements can reduce the uncertainty in an average caused by random variation, but cannot by itself remove a systematic bias.\n\nState an uncertainty with its method. For an analogue ruler, half the smallest division is a common reading estimate, but alignment and edge placement can contribute more. A digital last-digit resolution is only one part of the total uncertainty. Report a value and uncertainty at matching decimal places. When comparing with a reference, percent error describes relative disagreement; it does not identify its cause.',
 'relative uncertainty = absolute uncertainty / |measured value|\npercent uncertainty = relative uncertainty × 100%\npercent error = |experimental - reference| / |reference| × 100%',
 'A length is (25.0 ± 0.2) cm. A gravity experiment gives 9.60 m/s²; compare it with the assigned reference 9.81 m/s².',
 ['The length percent uncertainty is (0.2/25.0) × 100% = 0.8%.',
  'The gravity percent error is |9.60 - 9.81|/9.81 × 100% ≈ 2.1%.',
  'Agreement cannot be judged fully from percent error alone. Compare the discrepancy with the experiment’s uncertainty and discuss timing, height measurement, and model assumptions.'],
 'Avoid writing “human error” without identifying a mechanism. Reaction-time variation, parallax, and a ruler zero offset are specific sources that suggest specific improvements.',
 [('Readings are 10.1, 10.1, and 10.2 cm, but the reference is 11.0 cm. Describe the data.', 'They are relatively precise but inaccurate against that reference. Investigate calibration or a consistent method error.'),
  ('What is the percent uncertainty of (4.0 ± 0.1) s?', '0.1/4.0 × 100% = 2.5%.')])
lesson(u, 'Algebra and a repeatable problem-solving method', [('2-4',59)],
 'Rearrange a physical equation before substituting values and evaluate the result.',
 'A successful solution starts with a model: identify the object or system, choose axes, list the given quantities with units, and name the unknown. Sketch the situation before choosing an equation. Rearranging symbolically helps expose which quantities matter and catches errors that numerical substitution can hide. Every operation must affect both sides of the equation.\n\nAfter substituting SI values, carry units through the arithmetic. End with a signed answer or a magnitude and direction, as appropriate. Check whether the answer is physically plausible: a stopping time should be positive, a passive efficiency should not exceed 100%, and a component cannot have a magnitude larger than its vector. A quadratic equation may have two mathematical roots; decide which roots belong to the time interval and situation being studied.',
 'v = v₀ + at  →  t = (v - v₀)/a\nv² = v₀² + 2aΔx  →  Δx = (v² - v₀²)/(2a)',
 'A cart moves at +12.0 m/s and brakes at -3.00 m/s². Find the time and displacement until it first stops.',
 ['Choose the initial motion as positive. The stopping velocity is 0, and the stated acceleration is constant.',
  't = (0 - 12.0)/(-3.00) = 4.00 s.',
  'Δx = (0² - 12.0²)/(2 × -3.00) = +24.0 m. The cart keeps moving forward while slowing; it does not have negative displacement.'],
 'A negative acceleration does not always mean slowing down. It means the acceleration points along the negative axis. Do not take the square root of a velocity-squared result without considering direction.',
 [('Rearrange P = E/t for E.', 'Multiply both sides by t: E = Pt.'),
  ('A cart starts at -6.0 m/s with acceleration +2.0 m/s². When does it stop?', 't = [0 - (-6.0)]/2.0 = 3.0 s. Velocity and acceleration are opposite, so it slows.')])
lesson(u, 'Graphing, slope, and area', [('2-1',49),('2-2',54),('2-3',56),('2-6',65)],
 'Extract physical meaning and correct units from the slope or area of a graph.',
 'Label each axis with the quantity and unit, choose a scale that uses the available space, and plot measurements clearly. A best-fit line follows the trend of noisy data; it need not pass through every point. Use well-separated points on that line to calculate its slope. The slope unit is the vertical-axis unit divided by the horizontal-axis unit. A nonzero intercept can describe a real starting value or a systematic offset.\n\nOn a position-time graph, slope gives velocity. On a velocity-time graph, slope gives acceleration and signed area gives displacement. On an acceleration-time graph, signed area gives change in velocity. Area below the time axis is negative. Interpolation estimates within the measured range; extrapolation extends beyond it and relies more strongly on the model remaining valid.',
 'slope = rise/run = Δ(vertical quantity)/Δ(horizontal quantity)\nrectangle area = base × height\ntriangle area = ½ × base × height',
 'A velocity-time line rises from 2.0 m/s at 0 s to 8.0 m/s at 3.0 s. Find acceleration and displacement.',
 ['Slope: a = (8.0 - 2.0)/(3.0 - 0) = 2.0 m/s².',
  'Area: a 3.0 s × 2.0 m/s rectangle gives 6.0 m, and a ½ × 3.0 s × 6.0 m/s triangle gives 9.0 m.',
  'Total displacement is 15 m. The trapezoid method gives the same result: ½(2.0 + 8.0)(3.0) = 15 m.'],
 'The slope of a velocity-time graph is acceleration, not velocity. The height of a position-time graph is position, not speed. Do not automatically connect noisy experimental points with a zigzag line.',
 [('A position-time line falls 6.0 m over 2.0 s. What is its velocity?', 'v = -6.0/2.0 = -3.0 m/s.'),
  ('A constant acceleration of -2.0 m/s² lasts 3.0 s. What does the graph area represent?', 'Δv = (-2.0)(3.0) = -6.0 m/s, a change in velocity rather than a displacement.')])
lesson(u, 'Trigonometry and vector components', [('3-1',76),('3-2',82)],
 'Resolve a vector into signed perpendicular components and reconstruct its magnitude and direction.',
 'A scalar has magnitude; a vector has magnitude and direction. Speed, mass, time, and energy are scalars. Displacement, velocity, acceleration, and force are vectors. A component is the signed projection of a vector along an axis. When the angle is measured counterclockwise from the positive x-axis, the x-component uses cosine and the y-component uses sine. If the angle is measured from another axis, identify adjacent and opposite sides again.\n\nAdd vectors by adding x-components separately from y-components. Subtraction means adding the reversed vector. Use the Pythagorean theorem to reconstruct the magnitude. The inverse tangent gives a reference angle; the component signs determine the quadrant. In this course set the calculator to degrees for stated degree angles.',
 'Aₓ = A cos θ; Aᵧ = A sin θ\nRₓ = Aₓ + Bₓ; Rᵧ = Aᵧ + Bᵧ\nR = √(Rₓ² + Rᵧ²)\ntan θ = Rᵧ/Rₓ  (check quadrant)',
 'A 20.0 N force acts 30.0° above the horizontal. Resolve it. Then find the resultant of 3.0 m east and 4.0 m north.',
 ['Fₓ = 20.0 cos 30.0° = 17.3 N; Fᵧ = 20.0 sin 30.0° = 10.0 N. Both components are positive.',
  'The displacement components are Rₓ = 3.0 m and Rᵧ = 4.0 m, so R = √(3.0² + 4.0²) = 5.0 m.',
  'θ = tan⁻¹(4.0/3.0) ≈ 53° north of east. The walked distance is 7.0 m, which differs from the displacement magnitude.'],
 'Do not add vector magnitudes unless the vectors are collinear in the same direction. Always state the axis from which an angle is measured.',
 [('Find the components of 10.0 m at 150° from +x.', 'Aₓ = -8.66 m and Aᵧ = +5.00 m; the vector lies in quadrant II.'),
  ('What is the resultant of 8 N east and 3 N west?', 'Take east as positive: 8 + (-3) = 5 N east.')])

u = unit(2, 'Motion', 'Describe one-dimensional motion with words, vectors, graphs, and constant-acceleration equations.',
 'Ch. 2; Ch. 3 §§3-1, 3-2',
 'Record a toy cart or walking motion against a measured background. Use several equally spaced times to build a position-time table, then estimate interval velocities. Identify a nearly uniform segment and an accelerating segment. Explain the effect of video frame rate, camera angle, and position resolution on the conclusion. Never infer constant acceleration from only two positions.')
lesson(u, 'Position, displacement, distance, and average velocity', [('2-1',49)],
 'Distinguish the length of a path from a signed change in position.',
 'Choose an origin and a positive direction. Position x locates an object relative to that origin. Displacement Δx is final position minus initial position; it depends only on the endpoints. Distance is the total path length and is nonnegative. A trip that returns to its start has zero displacement, but can have a large distance.\n\nAverage velocity is displacement divided by elapsed time. Average speed is distance divided by elapsed time. These can differ whenever direction changes. The time interval must include the whole trip being described. Changing the origin shifts both positions by the same amount and leaves displacement unchanged; reversing the positive axis reverses the signs of vector components.',
 'Δx = x_f - x_i\nv_avg = Δx/Δt\nspeed_avg = total distance/Δt',
 'A student walks from x = 2.0 m to x = 12.0 m, then to x = 5.0 m. The full trip takes 8.0 s.',
 ['Displacement: 5.0 - 2.0 = +3.0 m.',
  'Distance: |12.0 - 2.0| + |5.0 - 12.0| = 10.0 + 7.0 = 17.0 m.',
  'Average velocity is +3.0/8.0 = +0.38 m/s to two significant figures. Average speed is 17.0/8.0 = 2.1 m/s.'],
 'A zero average velocity does not mean the object stayed still. Distance and displacement have the same unit but describe different quantities.',
 [('A cyclist completes a 400 m loop in 80 s. Find average velocity and speed.', 'The displacement is zero, so average velocity is 0 m/s. Average speed is 400/80 = 5.0 m/s.'),
  ('An object moves from -3 m to -8 m. Find its displacement.', 'Δx = -8 - (-3) = -5 m. Its final position and displacement are both negative here, but they are different quantities.')])
lesson(u, 'Instantaneous velocity and interpreting position graphs', [('2-2',54)],
 'Use a local graph slope to infer velocity and recognize a turning point.',
 'Average velocity describes an interval. Instantaneous velocity describes motion at a particular moment and is estimated by the slope of a tangent to a position-time curve. A steep tangent means a large speed; an upward tangent means positive velocity and a downward tangent means negative velocity. A horizontal tangent means zero instantaneous velocity.\n\nA straight position-time line describes constant velocity. A curved line indicates changing velocity. At a smooth maximum or minimum of position, the velocity can be zero as the object reverses direction. A flat interval means the position stays fixed throughout that interval. Two objects at an intersection of their position-time curves are at the same position at that time, but need not have the same velocity.',
 'v at an instant ≈ slope of tangent to x-t graph\nspeed = |v|',
 'A tangent at t = 4.0 s passes through the graph points (2.0 s, 1.0 m) and (6.0 s, 9.0 m). Find the instantaneous velocity at 4.0 s.',
 ['Use points on the tangent line, not two widely separated points on the curved trajectory.',
  'v = (9.0 - 1.0)/(6.0 - 2.0) = +2.0 m/s.',
  'This is the local velocity estimate at 4.0 s. The positive sign describes direction; the instantaneous speed is 2.0 m/s.'],
 'Position being zero says only that the object is at the origin. It does not imply zero velocity. A graph can slope downward while all its position values remain positive.',
 [('A position-time graph is horizontal at x = 5 m from 2 to 6 s. Describe the motion.', 'The object rests at x = 5 m throughout that interval; v = 0.'),
  ('A curve rises but becomes less steep. Is the object speeding up?', 'No. Its positive velocity decreases in magnitude, so it slows while continuing in the positive direction.')])
lesson(u, 'Acceleration and the signs of motion', [('2-3',56)],
 'Calculate acceleration and decide whether speed increases or decreases.',
 'Acceleration is the rate at which velocity changes. Its unit m/s² means that the velocity changes by a certain number of m/s each second. Average acceleration uses the total velocity change over an interval; instantaneous acceleration is the tangent slope on a velocity-time graph.\n\nIn one dimension, an object speeds up when velocity and acceleration have the same sign and slows when they have opposite signs. A negative acceleration can therefore speed up an object moving negatively. At the moment a ball turns around, its velocity can be zero while its acceleration remains nonzero. A change in direction is also a change in velocity even if the speed before and after has the same magnitude.',
 'a_avg = (v_f - v_i)/Δt\nΔv = v_f - v_i',
 'A cart changes velocity from -4.0 m/s to -10.0 m/s in 3.0 s. Find acceleration and describe its speed.',
 ['Δv = -10.0 - (-4.0) = -6.0 m/s.',
  'a_avg = -6.0/3.0 = -2.0 m/s².',
  'Its speed increases from 4.0 to 10.0 m/s. Both velocity and acceleration point along the negative axis.'],
 'Avoid treating “deceleration” as automatically negative. Use a chosen axis and signed velocity and acceleration. A zero velocity at one instant is not proof of zero acceleration.',
 [('Velocity changes from +5 m/s to -5 m/s in 2 s. Find average acceleration.', 'Δv = -10 m/s; a_avg = -5 m/s². Equal initial and final speeds do not mean zero acceleration.'),
  ('v = -6 m/s and a = +2 m/s². Is the object speeding up at this instant?', 'It is slowing because the signs are opposite. It may later stop and reverse if the acceleration continues.')])
lesson(u, 'Constant-acceleration equations and their derivation', [('2-4',59)],
 'Choose and apply a kinematic equation under the constant-acceleration assumption.',
 'For constant acceleration, the velocity-time graph is a straight line. Its slope gives v = v₀ + at. Displacement is the area under that line: a rectangle v₀t plus a triangle ½at². This gives Δx = v₀t + ½at². The same area is a trapezoid with average height (v₀ + v)/2. Eliminating time between the equations produces v² = v₀² + 2aΔx.\n\nList the five quantities v₀, v, a, t, and Δx. Select an equation containing the unknown and the known quantities while omitting a quantity that is unavailable. These equations apply to a single interval of constant acceleration. Divide a trip into intervals when acceleration changes, and carry the ending velocity and position of one interval into the next.',
 'v = v₀ + at\nΔx = v₀t + ½at²\nv² = v₀² + 2aΔx\nΔx = ½(v₀ + v)t\nΔx = vt - ½at²',
 'A bicycle starts at 3.0 m/s and accelerates uniformly at 1.5 m/s² for 4.0 s. Find final velocity and displacement.',
 ['v = 3.0 + (1.5)(4.0) = 9.0 m/s.',
  'Δx = (3.0)(4.0) + ½(1.5)(4.0)² = 12 + 12 = 24 m.',
  'Check with average velocity: ½(3.0 + 9.0)(4.0) = 24 m. The two methods agree because acceleration is constant.'],
 'The arithmetic mean of initial and final velocities is not generally the average velocity when acceleration varies. Δx is signed displacement, not automatically distance.',
 [('A car goes from rest to 20.0 m/s in 5.00 s with constant acceleration. Find a and Δx.', 'a = 4.00 m/s²; Δx = ½(0 + 20.0)(5.00) = 50.0 m.'),
  ('A cart has v₀ = 10.0 m/s, a = -2.00 m/s², and Δx = 16.0 m before turning. Find v.', 'v² = 100 - 64 = 36.0. Before turning it still moves positively, so v = +6.00 m/s.')])
lesson(u, 'Signed graph areas and multi-stage trips', [('2-6',65)],
 'Calculate displacement and distance separately from a velocity-time graph.',
 'A velocity-time graph describes direction through its sign. The signed area above the time axis adds positive displacement; area below adds negative displacement. Total distance is the sum of the magnitudes of those areas. When a straight segment crosses zero velocity, split the area at the crossing before finding distance.\n\nFor multi-stage motion, keep a running record of time, position, and velocity. A moving object can travel far but finish near its start. An acceleration-time graph has a different area meaning: its area changes velocity. Starting velocity is needed to reconstruct a velocity graph from an acceleration graph, and starting position is needed to reconstruct position from velocity.',
 'Δx = signed area under v-t graph\ndistance = area under |v|-t graph\nΔv = signed area under a-t graph',
 'An object travels at +4.0 m/s for 3.0 s, then at -2.0 m/s for 2.0 s. Find its displacement, distance, and average velocity.',
 ['The two displacements are (+4.0)(3.0) = +12 m and (-2.0)(2.0) = -4.0 m.',
  'Net displacement is +8.0 m. Distance is 12 + 4.0 = 16 m.',
  'The full elapsed time is 5.0 s, so average velocity is +8.0/5.0 = +1.6 m/s. The idealized abrupt reversal represents two separate intervals.'],
 'Do not cancel negative graph areas when calculating distance. Do not confuse the area under a position graph with displacement.',
 [('Velocity falls linearly from +6 m/s to -6 m/s over 4 s. Find displacement and distance.', 'The two triangles each have magnitude ½(2)(6) = 6 m. Displacement is 0; distance is 12 m.'),
  ('a = +3 m/s² for 2 s, starting at v₀ = -1 m/s. Find v.', 'Δv = 6 m/s. v = -1 + 6 = +5 m/s.')])

u = unit(3, 'Projectiles', 'Separate horizontal and vertical motion, then reconnect them through a shared flight time.',
 'Ch. 2 §2-5; Ch. 4 §§4-1 to 4-4',
 'Use a soft ball rolling off a low surface, with an adult or teacher supervising the setup. Predict its landing distance from height and horizontal launch speed. Record several trials and compare with the no-drag model. Discuss uncertainty in the launch point and speed. An alternative is to collect simulated data from the trajectory explorer and clearly label it as model data, not an experiment.')
lesson(u, 'Vertical motion and free fall', [('2-5',63)],
 'Solve upward and downward motion with a consistent sign convention.',
 'Free fall means gravity is the only significant force after release. Near Earth’s surface, use approximately constant downward acceleration g = 9.81 m/s² unless the problem assigns another value. If upward is positive, aᵧ = -g for the entire flight: ascent, peak, and descent. Mass does not appear in the ideal kinematic equations.\n\nAn upward-moving ball slows because its velocity and acceleration oppose one another. At the highest point vᵧ = 0, but aᵧ remains -g. During descent both are negative, so the ball speeds up. Use the displacement between the stated initial and final levels, and do not assume that the landing level equals the launch level.',
 'vᵧ = v₀ᵧ - gt\nΔy = v₀ᵧt - ½gt²\nvᵧ² = v₀ᵧ² - 2gΔy',
 'A ball is thrown straight up at 14.0 m/s. Find the time to the peak and the height gained.',
 ['At the peak, vᵧ = 0. Thus t = 14.0/9.81 = 1.43 s.',
  '0 = 14.0² - 2(9.81)Δy, giving Δy = 9.99 m.',
  'If it later returns to the launch level with negligible drag, the total time is 2.85 s and the returning velocity is -14.0 m/s. The symmetry applies only between equal heights.'],
 'Gravity does not switch off at the peak. The acceleration does not change sign when the ball changes direction. Real air resistance can spoil the mass independence and symmetry.',
 [('A ball is dropped from rest through 20.0 m. Find fall time and impact speed.', 'With upward positive, -20.0 = -½gt², so t = 2.02 s. vᵧ = -19.8 m/s and speed is 19.8 m/s.'),
  ('At the highest point of a vertical throw, what are v and a?', 'v = 0 for that instant, while a = 9.81 m/s² downward in the ideal model.')])
lesson(u, 'Independent components and horizontal launches', [('4-3',103),('4-4',106)],
 'Find flight time vertically and range horizontally for a horizontal launch.',
 'A projectile is an object moving after launch under gravity alone in the ideal model. Resolve its motion into perpendicular axes. Gravity supplies vertical acceleration but no horizontal acceleration, so the horizontal velocity remains constant while the vertical velocity changes. Both components use the same time because they describe the same object.\n\nFor a horizontal launch, v₀ᵧ = 0. Use the height to determine the time to land, then multiply that time by the horizontal velocity. A ball dropped and a ball launched horizontally from the same height land together under the ideal assumptions, even though their horizontal positions differ. The initial push is not a force that continues acting throughout flight.',
 'aₓ = 0; aᵧ = -g\nΔx = v₀ₓt\nΔy = v₀ᵧt - ½gt²\nfor horizontal launch from height h: t = √(2h/g)',
 'A ball leaves a 1.25 m high table horizontally at 4.00 m/s. How far from the edge does it land?',
 ['Set the launch point at y = 0 and the floor at y = -1.25 m.',
  'Vertical motion gives -1.25 = -½(9.81)t², so t = 0.505 s.',
  'Δx = (4.00)(0.505) = 2.02 m. The horizontal speed changes the range, not the fall time in this model.'],
 'Do not use the total launch speed as a vertical initial speed. Do not invent a horizontal acceleration when air resistance is neglected.',
 [('A horizontal launch speed doubles from the same height. What happens to flight time and range?', 'Flight time is unchanged; range doubles.'),
  ('A ball launches horizontally at 6.0 m/s from 5.0 m high. Find the range.', 't = √(10.0/9.81) ≈ 1.01 s; range ≈ 6.1 m.')])
lesson(u, 'Angled launches and equal-height range', [('4-4',106)],
 'Resolve an angled launch and identify when the shortcut range formula applies.',
 'For launch speed v₀ at angle θ above horizontal, use v₀ₓ = v₀ cos θ and v₀ᵧ = v₀ sin θ. Vertical motion determines when the object reaches a chosen height. Horizontal motion then determines where it is. At the peak, only the vertical component is zero; an angled projectile normally retains horizontal speed.\n\nIf landing and launch heights are equal, the nonzero root of Δy = 0 gives t = 2v₀ sin θ/g. Substitution into Δx = v₀ cos θ × t gives R = v₀² sin(2θ)/g. The ideal range is largest at 45°, and complementary launch angles have equal ranges. These conclusions require equal heights, fixed launch speed, uniform gravity, and negligible drag.',
 'v₀ₓ = v₀ cos θ; v₀ᵧ = v₀ sin θ\nt_flight = 2v₀ sin θ/g  (equal heights)\nR = v₀² sin(2θ)/g  (equal heights)\nh_max above launch = v₀² sin²θ/(2g)',
 'Launch a ball at 20.0 m/s and 30.0°. It lands at the launch height. Find time, range, and maximum height.',
 ['v₀ₓ = 17.3 m/s and v₀ᵧ = 10.0 m/s.',
  'Flight time is 2(10.0)/9.81 = 2.04 s; range is 17.3205 × 2.0387 = 35.3 m.',
  'The maximum height above launch is 10.0²/[2(9.81)] = 5.10 m. At the peak the speed is still 17.3 m/s.'],
 'The 45° maximum-range statement is conditional. Never apply the equal-height range formula to a cliff launch without checking the geometry.',
 [('For fixed speed at equal heights, compare 25° and 65° launches.', 'They have equal ideal ranges because sin 50° = sin 130°. The 65° launch has a greater height and flight time.'),
  ('A 10.0 m/s launch at 45.0° returns to its starting height. Find range.', 'R = 10.0² sin 90°/9.81 = 10.2 m.')])
lesson(u, 'Unequal launch and landing heights', [('4-4',106)],
 'Solve the vertical quadratic and select the physical flight time.',
 'When landing height differs from launch height, begin with the full vertical displacement equation. Rearrange it to a quadratic in time. The quadratic may describe crossings of a height both before and after the peak, or may produce a negative time outside the chosen motion interval. Read the roots in the context of the question.\n\nOnce time is known, find horizontal displacement using the horizontal component. Do not replace the vertical displacement by the distance travelled up and down; kinematics uses endpoints. If a projectile lands above its launch point, first check whether its maximum height is high enough to reach that level. A negative discriminant means the chosen height is unreachable within the ideal model.',
 '½gt² - v₀ᵧt + Δy = 0\nt = [v₀ᵧ ± √(v₀ᵧ² - 2gΔy)]/g\nΔx = v₀ₓt',
 'A ball leaves a platform 10.0 m above the ground at 15.0 m/s and 30.0° above horizontal. Find its landing distance.',
 ['Components: v₀ₓ = 15.0 cos 30.0° = 12.99 m/s; v₀ᵧ = 7.50 m/s. The ground has Δy = -10.0 m.',
  'Solve 4.905t² - 7.50t - 10.0 = 0. Roots are about 2.38 s and -0.855 s.',
  'The future landing uses t = 2.38 s. Range is 12.99 × 2.385 ≈ 31.0 m. The negative root is outside the flight after launch.'],
 'Landing below the start means Δy is negative if up is positive. Do not automatically reject one of two positive roots: they may represent ascent and descent through the same height.',
 [('A ball is launched vertically at 10.0 m/s. Can it reach 6.0 m above launch?', 'No. h_max = 100/(2 × 9.81) = 5.10 m, which is below 6.0 m.'),
  ('A horizontal projectile lands 20.0 m away after 2.00 s. Find launch speed and drop height.', 'v₀ₓ = 20.0/2.00 = 10.0 m/s. h = ½(9.81)(2.00)² = 19.6 m.')])
lesson(u, 'Velocity during flight and evaluating the model', [('4-2',100),('4-4',106)],
 'Reconstruct the velocity vector at a chosen time and explain ideal-model limitations.',
 'The velocity vector points tangent to the trajectory. Its horizontal component stays constant in the ideal model and its vertical component changes linearly. Use the components to calculate the speed and an angle above or below horizontal. The trajectory curves because the direction of velocity changes, while acceleration remains downward.\n\nThe textbook’s projectile model treats the object as a particle and neglects air resistance. It works best when drag and lift are small relative to weight. A feather, a spinning ball, and a fast lightweight object can differ substantially from this model. An experiment should report those limitations, separate uncertainty in measurements from systematic model disagreement, and avoid claiming that a single trial proves the ideal law.',
 'vₓ = v₀ₓ\nvᵧ = v₀ᵧ - gt\nspeed = √(vₓ² + vᵧ²)\nvelocity angle: tan φ = vᵧ/vₓ  (check signs)',
 'A horizontal projectile has v₀ₓ = 8.00 m/s. Find its velocity 0.600 s after launch, assuming it is still airborne.',
 ['vₓ = +8.00 m/s; vᵧ = 0 - (9.81)(0.600) = -5.89 m/s.',
  'Speed is √(8.00² + 5.886²) = 9.93 m/s.',
  'φ = tan⁻¹(5.886/8.00) = 36.3° below horizontal. State both magnitude and direction to specify the velocity.'],
 'The horizontal velocity is not the whole speed except when vᵧ = 0. A trajectory drawing is a position picture, not a velocity-time graph.',
 [('What is acceleration at the peak of an angled ideal projectile?', 'aₓ = 0 and aᵧ = -g; the full acceleration is downward and nonzero.'),
  ('What observation would suggest drag matters?', 'A systematic decline of horizontal speed after release, beyond measurement uncertainty, contradicts the no-drag assumption.')])

u = unit(4, 'Forces', 'Use interactions and free-body diagrams to explain motion, contact forces, friction, and apparent weight.',
 'Ch. 5; Ch. 6 §6-1; Ch. 7 §7-4',
 'Pull a block with a spring scale on a level surface. Record the largest force just before sliding and the force during nearly constant-speed sliding. Change the load while keeping the surfaces the same. Plot friction versus normal force and discuss the dry-friction model. Use small loads and keep hands clear of falling masses. Predict before measuring; explain a specific source of uncertainty.')
lesson(u, 'Newton’s first and second laws', [('5-1',130)],
 'Relate net force to acceleration and distinguish balanced motion from rest.',
 'Newton’s first law says an object maintains constant velocity when its net external force is zero in an inertial frame. Constant velocity includes rest and straight-line motion at a steady speed. Mass measures inertia: more mass requires more net force for the same acceleration. A force is an interaction with another object, not something an object carries because it moves.\n\nNewton’s second law relates the vector sum of external forces to acceleration. Add forces by components before dividing by mass. A single force can be large while the net force is zero because another force balances it. These introductory calculations assume constant mass and an approximately inertial frame; Earth’s surface is an adequate approximation for the examples here.',
 'ΣF⃗ = ma⃗\nΣFₓ = maₓ; ΣFᵧ = maᵧ\n1 N = 1 kg·m/s²',
 'A 4.00 kg cart is pulled right with 18.0 N and left with 6.00 N. Find acceleration.',
 ['Choose right as positive. The horizontal net force is 18.0 - 6.00 = +12.0 N.',
  'aₓ = 12.0/4.00 = +3.00 m/s².',
  'If the cart initially moves left, this rightward acceleration initially slows it. Force gives acceleration, not the current velocity.'],
 'Constant speed along a straight line does not require a forward net force. In ordinary motion a driving force may be needed to balance resistance, leaving the net force zero.',
 [('A 2.0 kg object has acceleration -3.0 m/s². Find net force.', 'ΣFₓ = (2.0)(-3.0) = -6.0 N.'),
  ('Can an object move when its net force is zero?', 'Yes. It can maintain a nonzero constant velocity. Zero net force means zero acceleration, not necessarily zero velocity.')])
lesson(u, 'Free-body diagrams and interaction pairs', [('5-1',130),('5-3',142)],
 'Draw only forces acting on the chosen object and identify Newton’s third-law pairs.',
 'Choose one object, represent it as a dot or simple outline, and draw each external force with its tail on that object. Label the source of each force: Earth supplies gravity, a surface supplies normal force or friction, a rope supplies tension, and a spring supplies spring force. Add coordinate axes chosen to simplify the problem. Velocity and acceleration can be shown separately, but they are not forces.\n\nNewton’s third law states that the force of A on B is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the force of B on A. The pair acts on different objects, so it does not cancel in the free-body diagram of either object alone. Weight and normal force on the same block are not a third-law pair even when they balance.',
 'F⃗_A on B = -F⃗_B on A\nΣFₓ = maₓ; ΣFᵧ = maᵧ',
 'Draw the forces on a book resting on a table and identify the partner of each force.',
 ['On the book: gravity mg downward from Earth and normal force N upward from the table. There is no horizontal force if no horizontal interaction is present.',
  'Because aᵧ = 0, N - mg = 0. Their equality comes from equilibrium, not the third law.',
  'The partner of Earth’s force on the book is the book’s gravitational force on Earth. The partner of the table’s normal force on the book is the book’s contact force on the table.'],
 'Do not put the book’s force on the table into the book’s own free-body diagram. Do not add an extra “motion force” or “ma force” to the diagram.',
 [('A truck and a small car interact during a collision. Which exerts the larger interaction force?', 'Their interaction forces have equal magnitudes and opposite directions. Their accelerations can differ because their masses differ.'),
  ('Why do weight and normal force not form an action-reaction pair?', 'Both act on the same object. A third-law pair must act on two different objects.')])
lesson(u, 'Mass, gravity, and normal force', [('5-2',138)],
 'Calculate gravitational force and determine the normal force from vertical dynamics.',
 'Mass is measured in kilograms and remains the same when an object changes location. Gravitational force near Earth has magnitude mg and points downward. A normal force is perpendicular to a contact surface and arises because the surface resists penetration. It is not defined as “equal to weight.”\n\nFor a block on a horizontal surface with no vertical acceleration, the vertical forces must sum to zero. If an upward applied force partly supports the block, the normal force is reduced. If a downward applied force presses the block into the surface, it increases. A simple nonadhesive surface can push but cannot pull the object downward; a computed negative N indicates that the assumed contact has failed.',
 'F_g = mg\nlevel surface with upward applied force: N + F_up - mg = maᵧ\nfor aᵧ = 0: N = mg - F_up',
 'A 5.00 kg box on a level floor is pulled upward with 12.0 N while staying on the floor. Find the normal force.',
 ['Gravity is mg = (5.00)(9.81) = 49.05 N downward.',
  'Vertical acceleration is zero, so N + 12.0 - 49.05 = 0.',
  'N = 37.1 N after rounding. The floor provides the remaining support; it does not provide the full 49.1 N.'],
 'A scale commonly displays a mass-equivalent reading but responds to a support force. A normal force is not necessarily vertical: it is perpendicular to the surface.',
 [('A 2.00 kg block is pressed down with 10.0 N on a level floor. Find N.', 'N = mg + 10.0 = 29.6 N if the block has no vertical acceleration.'),
  ('A 3.0 kg object moves to a location where g = 1.6 m/s². Find its mass and gravitational force.', 'Mass remains 3.0 kg; gravitational force becomes 4.8 N.')])
lesson(u, 'Tension and spring forces', [('5-2',138),('7-4',195)],
 'Model an ideal rope and use Hooke’s law within a spring’s elastic range.',
 'A taut rope pulls an attached object along the rope; it does not push. In the ideal model a massless rope running over a massless frictionless pulley has one tension magnitude throughout. Real rope mass and pulley effects can make tensions differ, so state the idealization. A slack rope has no tension in this simple model.\n\nA spring displaced from its relaxed length exerts a restoring force. Hooke’s law Fₛ = -kx says that the force opposes the signed extension or compression x. The spring constant k, measured in N/m, describes stiffness. This proportional model applies within an appropriate elastic range, not after permanent deformation or for every elastic object.',
 'Fₛ = -kx  (signed force along spring axis)\n|Fₛ| = k|x|\nat rest for a hanging mass: T = mg',
 'A vertical spring extends 0.0400 m when a 0.200 kg mass hangs at rest. Find its spring constant.',
 ['At equilibrium the upward spring force balances the downward gravitational force.',
  'kx = mg, so k = mg/x = (0.200)(9.81)/0.0400 = 49.1 N/m.',
  'Measure x from the relaxed spring length, not from its total stretched length. A force-extension graph has slope k when plotting the required holding-force magnitude.'],
 'Tension is not automatically equal to mg when a mass accelerates. The negative sign in Hooke’s law gives force direction; it does not mean that the spring constant is negative.',
 [('A spring has k = 200 N/m and extension +0.030 m. Find the signed restoring force.', 'Fₛ = -(200)(0.030) = -6.0 N along the chosen spring axis.'),
  ('A 1.00 kg hanging mass accelerates upward at 2.00 m/s². Find tension.', 'T - mg = ma, so T = m(g + a) = 11.8 N.')])
lesson(u, 'Static and kinetic friction', [('6-1',160)],
 'Choose the correct friction model and determine friction before assuming motion.',
 'Dry friction acts along the contact surface. Static friction prevents relative sliding and adjusts to the force required for equilibrium, up to a maximum μₛN. Kinetic friction acts when surfaces slide relative to one another, and its magnitude is approximated by μₖN. Usually μₛ exceeds μₖ for the same pair of surfaces, but actual friction depends on conditions.\n\nTo decide whether a block moves, first calculate the static friction needed to keep it at rest, then compare with the maximum. If the required force exceeds that limit, switch to the kinetic model. Friction opposes relative sliding or its tendency; it does not always oppose an object’s overall motion. For example, static friction can propel a walking person or a driven tire forward.',
 '0 ≤ fₛ ≤ μₛN\nfₛ,max = μₛN\nfₖ = μₖN',
 'A 10.0 kg box has μₛ = 0.40 and μₖ = 0.30 on a level floor. Compare horizontal pushes of 25 N and 50 N.',
 ['N = mg = 98.1 N. Maximum static friction is 0.40 × 98.1 = 39.24 N.',
  'For the 25 N push from rest, static friction is 25 N in the opposite direction and acceleration is zero.',
  'A 50 N push exceeds the static limit. Once sliding, fₖ = 29.43 N, so a = (50 - 29.43)/10.0 ≈ 2.1 m/s².'],
 'The formula μₛN is the maximum static friction, not its automatic value. Calculate N from the actual forces; an angled pull can change it.',
 [('A resting box needs 8 N of friction, while μₛN = 12 N. What is fₛ?', 'fₛ = 8 N. The maximum is 12 N but the actual required force is smaller.'),
  ('A sliding block has N = 40 N and μₖ = 0.25. Find kinetic friction.', 'fₖ = 10 N, directed opposite the block’s sliding relative to the surface.')])
lesson(u, 'Elevators and apparent weight', [('5-2',138),('5-3',142)],
 'Predict a scale’s support force from acceleration rather than direction of travel.',
 'A person standing on a scale experiences gravity downward and a normal force upward. The scale responds to N, called apparent weight. With upward positive, N - mg = maᵧ. Upward acceleration makes N greater than mg; downward acceleration makes it smaller. Constant velocity in either direction gives N = mg.\n\nAn elevator travelling upward can have downward acceleration when it brakes, so the person then feels lighter. During ideal free fall the scale and person fall together; N becomes zero even though gravity remains present. Weightlessness in this sense means lack of support force, not absence of gravity. These statements use the ground as the approximately inertial reference frame.',
 'N - mg = maᵧ\nN = m(g + aᵧ)\nfree fall: aᵧ = -g → N = 0',
 'A 60.0 kg person is in an elevator moving upward but slowing with a downward acceleration of 1.50 m/s². Find the scale force.',
 ['Choose upward positive. The acceleration is aᵧ = -1.50 m/s² even though velocity points up.',
  'N = 60.0(9.81 - 1.50) = 498.6 N ≈ 499 N.',
  'The ordinary support force is 589 N. The smaller reading follows from downward acceleration. Mass is still 60.0 kg.'],
 'Do not infer acceleration from the word “upward” alone. Read whether speed is increasing, decreasing, or constant.',
 [('A 50.0 kg person accelerates upward at 2.00 m/s². Find N.', 'N = 50.0(9.81 + 2.00) = 591 N.'),
  ('An elevator moves downward at constant speed. How does N compare with mg?', 'N = mg because the acceleration is zero.')])

u = unit(5, '2D Forces & Simple Machines', 'Resolve forces on ramps, analyze connected objects, and explain how simple machines exchange force for distance.',
 'Ch. 3 §3-1; Ch. 5 §5-3; Ch. 6 §6-1; Ch. 12 §§12-1, 12-2; Ch. 7 §§7-2, 7-6',
 'Compare lifting a small load vertically with pulling it up a gentle ramp. Measure force and travel distance, then compare input work with the change in gravitational potential energy. Research an example of a simple machine using a publicly shared source from a local First Nation or an Indigenous-led organization. Cite the community and source, respect sharing protocols, and explain the mechanical principle without treating diverse First Peoples as one culture.')
lesson(u, 'Angled forces and equilibrium', [('3-1',76),('5-3',142)],
 'Apply Newton’s second law independently along two axes.',
 'In two dimensions the same vector law becomes two scalar equations. Resolve every angled force along the chosen axes, keeping signs. If the object is in equilibrium, both acceleration components are zero, so both component sums vanish. Two forces with cancelling horizontal components can still have vertical components that add.\n\nFor a box pulled upward at an angle on a horizontal floor, the upward pull reduces N. If the pull points downward it increases N. This also changes friction. Work from the free-body diagram rather than memorizing N = mg. The angle in a component formula must refer to the axis that defines the adjacent side.',
 'ΣFₓ = maₓ; ΣFᵧ = maᵧ\nFₓ = F cos θ; Fᵧ = F sin θ\nlevel floor, aᵧ = 0, upward pull: N = mg - F sin θ',
 'A 10.0 kg box is pulled with 40.0 N at 30.0° above horizontal on a frictionless level floor. Find N and horizontal acceleration.',
 ['Fₓ = 40.0 cos 30.0° = 34.6 N; Fᵧ = 20.0 N upward.',
  'Vertical balance gives N = 98.1 - 20.0 = 78.1 N.',
  'Horizontal dynamics gives aₓ = 34.641/10.0 = 3.46 m/s². The smaller N would also reduce friction if friction were present.'],
 'Do not use the full angled force in both component equations. Components reconstruct one force; they are not extra forces to add alongside it.',
 [('Two equal ropes make 30° with the horizontal and hold a 20.0 N load symmetrically. Find each tension.', 'Horizontal components cancel. 2T sin 30° = 20.0 N gives T = 20.0 N.'),
  ('What equations describe translational equilibrium?', 'ΣFₓ = 0 and ΣFᵧ = 0. An extended body may also require zero net torque for rotational equilibrium.')])
lesson(u, 'Inclined planes without friction', [('5-3',142),('6-1',160)],
 'Choose axes along a ramp and resolve gravity relative to those axes.',
 'For a ramp tilted by θ above horizontal, choose x down the ramp and y perpendicular outward. Gravity is vertical, but its components in these rotated axes are mg sin θ down the ramp and mg cos θ into the ramp. This choice avoids resolving the normal force: it lies entirely along the perpendicular axis.\n\nIf the block remains on a straight fixed ramp, its perpendicular acceleration is zero. With no other perpendicular force, N = mg cos θ. On a frictionless ramp the downslope equation gives a = g sin θ, independent of mass. The expression becomes zero for a level ramp and approaches g for a nearly vertical ramp, useful limiting checks.',
 'F_g,parallel = mg sin θ\nF_g,perpendicular = mg cos θ\nN = mg cos θ  (no other perpendicular forces)\na_down = g sin θ  (frictionless)',
 'A 4.00 kg block slides down a frictionless 25.0° ramp. Find N and acceleration.',
 ['Perpendicular balance gives N = (4.00)(9.81) cos 25.0° = 35.6 N.',
  'Downslope gravity is (4.00)(9.81) sin 25.0° = 16.6 N.',
  'a = 16.584/4.00 = 4.15 m/s² down the ramp. Gravity still points vertically; the components are bookkeeping along rotated axes.'],
 'N is not mg on a ramp. Do not draw mg sin θ and mg cos θ as extra forces in addition to mg; show either the original force or a clear component decomposition.',
 [('What is acceleration down a frictionless 30.0° ramp?', 'a = 9.81 sin 30.0° = 4.91 m/s².'),
  ('Does doubling block mass double the ramp acceleration?', 'No. Both the downslope gravitational force and inertia double, leaving a = g sin θ unchanged.')])
lesson(u, 'Friction on ramps and the threshold of slipping', [('6-1',160)],
 'Decide whether a block remains at rest and calculate acceleration after sliding begins.',
 'A resting block on a ramp tends to slide down because of mg sin θ. Static friction acts up the ramp to prevent that tendency if it can supply enough force. Under the simple ramp model, the threshold is mg sin θ = μₛmg cos θ, giving tan θ = μₛ. Below the threshold, actual friction is mg sin θ rather than its maximum.\n\nIf a block slides down, kinetic friction points up the ramp and the acceleration along the downward axis is g(sin θ - μₖ cos θ). If it slides up, kinetic friction points down, so gravity and friction both oppose the upward velocity. The direction of kinetic friction comes from the current sliding direction, not from a memorized picture.',
 'rest possible if mg sin θ ≤ μₛmg cos θ\ntan θ_threshold = μₛ\nsliding down: a = g(sin θ - μₖ cos θ)',
 'A block has μₛ = 0.50 and μₖ = 0.30 on a 30.0° ramp. Released from rest, does it slide? Find its acceleration if it does.',
 ['tan 30.0° = 0.577, which exceeds μₛ = 0.50, so static friction cannot hold it.',
  'Once sliding down, a = 9.81[sin 30.0° - 0.30 cos 30.0°].',
  'a = 2.36 m/s² down the ramp. The static coefficient determines the onset, while the kinetic coefficient determines this sliding acceleration.'],
 'Do not apply kinetic friction before checking whether the object slides. A downslope-moving block can slow down on a shallow ramp if friction exceeds the downslope weight component.',
 [('Find the threshold angle if μₛ = 0.40.', 'θ = tan⁻¹(0.40) = 21.8° in this ideal dry-friction model.'),
  ('A block slides upward on a ramp. Which direction does friction act?', 'Down the ramp, opposing upward sliding. Gravity’s parallel component also points down.')])
lesson(u, 'Connected masses and ideal pulleys', [('5-3',142)],
 'Write one equation per body and use the rope constraint to solve a system.',
 'For a taut, inextensible rope over an ideal pulley, connected objects have matching acceleration magnitudes along the rope. Draw a separate free-body diagram for each mass. Choose a positive direction for each that agrees with one assumed system motion. The same tension magnitude appears in both equations under the massless-rope, massless-pulley, frictionless assumptions.\n\nAdding the equations eliminates internal tension and gives the acceleration of the system. Substitute back to find tension. If the calculated acceleration is negative, the actual acceleration is opposite the assumed positive directions. Tension can differ from either mass’s weight because the masses accelerate.',
 'Atwood system, m₂ > m₁:\nm₂g - T = m₂a\nT - m₁g = m₁a\na = (m₂ - m₁)g/(m₁ + m₂)',
 'An ideal pulley connects m₁ = 2.00 kg and m₂ = 3.00 kg. Find acceleration and rope tension.',
 ['Assume m₂ accelerates down and m₁ up. Adding their equations gives (3.00 - 2.00)g = (3.00 + 2.00)a.',
  'a = 1.962 m/s², so m₂ accelerates downward at 1.96 m/s².',
  'T = m₁(g + a) = 2.00(9.81 + 1.962) = 23.5 N. Check m₂: m₂(g - a) gives the same tension.'],
 'The two masses have the same acceleration magnitude, not the same upward-signed acceleration. Keep each body’s chosen axis explicit. Do not set T equal to mg for an accelerating mass.',
 [('A 2.00 kg cart on a frictionless table connects to a 1.00 kg hanging mass. Find a and T.', 'For the cart T = 2a; for the hanging mass 9.81 - T = a. Thus a = 3.27 m/s² and T = 6.54 N.'),
  ('In an ideal Atwood system with equal masses and no other forces, what is a?', 'a = 0. The masses may remain at rest or move at constant speed if already moving.')])
lesson(u, 'Simple machines, mechanical advantage, and levers', [('7-2',187),('7-6',202),('12-1',363),('12-2',368)],
 'Relate force advantage to travel distance and use torque balance for an ideal lever.',
 'A simple machine changes the magnitude or direction of an applied force. Ramps, levers, pulleys, wedges, screws, and wheels with axles exchange force for distance. A smaller input force normally acts through a larger distance; an ideal machine conserves work rather than creating energy. Actual friction and deformation reduce useful output.\n\nMechanical advantage is output force divided by input force. Ideal mechanical advantage is input distance divided by output distance. A ramp has ideal advantage L/h. A frictionless movable pulley supporting a load with n equivalent vertical rope segments has ideal advantage n, and the pulling end must move n times the load’s rise. For a lever at rest, torque balance uses force times perpendicular moment arm. This is an algebra-level application of the textbook’s static equilibrium chapter.',
 'MA = F_out/F_in\nIMA = d_in/d_out\nideal ramp: IMA = L/h\nτ = F × perpendicular moment arm\nideal lever: F_in r_in = F_out r_out\nefficiency = (MA/IMA) × 100%',
 'An ideal lever has an input arm of 0.80 m and output arm of 0.20 m. What input force balances a 200 N load?',
 ['Torque balance: F_in(0.80) = (200)(0.20).',
  'F_in = 50 N, giving MA = 200/50 = 4.',
  'For a small rotation the input moves four times as far as the load. If the load rises 0.050 m, ideal input travel is 0.20 m; both works equal 10 J.'],
 'Mechanical advantage greater than one does not imply efficiency greater than 100%. Use perpendicular moment arms, not an arbitrary distance to a force’s application point.',
 [('A ramp is 3.0 m long and 0.60 m high. Find ideal advantage and ideal force for a 300 N load.', 'IMA = 3.0/0.60 = 5.0; ideal force = 300/5.0 = 60 N.'),
  ('A machine has IMA = 4.0 and MA = 3.0. Find efficiency.', 'Efficiency = (3.0/4.0) × 100% = 75%.')])

u = unit(6, 'Energy & Thermal Physics', 'Track work and energy in a defined system, calculate power and efficiency, and apply energy conservation to heating.',
 'Ch. 7; Ch. 8; Ch. 18 §§18-1, 18-4, 18-6',
 'Design an insulated warm-water/cool-water mixing investigation. Measure masses and initial temperatures, predict equilibrium temperature, then measure it. Account for the cup and thermometer if their heat capacities matter. Compare the prediction and data, identify heat leakage and timing limitations, and propose improvements. Use warm rather than boiling water. Relate insulation choices to an energy-efficient structure using a properly attributed, publicly shared community source.')
lesson(u, 'Work and force-displacement graphs', [('7-2',187),('7-5',198)],
 'Determine the sign and magnitude of work, including work by a changing force.',
 'Work is energy transfer by a force acting through displacement. For a constant force, only the component parallel to displacement contributes. A forward pull can do positive work, friction on a sliding block negative work, and a perpendicular normal force zero work along a level surface. Work is a scalar measured in joules.\n\nIf force changes with position, compute the signed area under the graph of the force component parallel to displacement. A rectangular area represents constant force; a triangular area represents a linear rise from zero. This graphical method gives the algebra-level meaning of the textbook’s integral. Holding a load stationary involves no mechanical work on that load because displacement is zero, even though a person uses metabolic energy.',
 'W = Fd cos θ  (constant force)\nW = signed area under F_parallel-versus-position graph\n1 J = 1 N·m',
 'A 30.0 N pull acts 60.0° above a horizontal displacement of 4.00 m. Separately, find work for a force rising linearly from 0 to 20.0 N over 0.500 m.',
 ['Constant-force work: W = (30.0)(4.00) cos 60.0° = +60.0 J.',
  'The perpendicular component does no work along the horizontal displacement.',
  'For the changing force, the triangular graph area is ½(0.500)(20.0) = 5.00 J. Using the final force for the whole distance would incorrectly give 10.0 J.'],
 'The angle belongs between force and displacement. A force being present is not sufficient for nonzero work on the chosen object.',
 [('A 10 N friction force opposes 3.0 m of sliding. Find its work on the block.', 'W = (10)(3.0) cos 180° = -30 J.'),
  ('What work does a normal force do during horizontal motion on a level rigid surface?', 'Zero in the simple model, because the normal force is perpendicular to displacement.')])
lesson(u, 'Kinetic energy and the work-energy theorem', [('7-1',185),('7-2',187)],
 'Use net work to calculate a change in speed without first finding time.',
 'Kinetic energy is the energy associated with motion. For speeds far below the speed of light, K = ½mv². Speed is squared, so kinetic energy is nonnegative and does not encode direction. Doubling speed quadruples kinetic energy at the same mass.\n\nThe net work on a particle equals its change in kinetic energy. Add the work of all forces, not just the applied force. Positive net work increases kinetic energy; negative net work reduces it. This method is useful when displacement and forces are known but time is not. It does not directly determine a velocity direction, so interpret the final speed using the motion and geometry.',
 'K = ½mv²\nW_net = ΔK = K_f - K_i\nv_f² = v_i² + 2W_net/m',
 'A 2.00 kg cart initially travels at 3.00 m/s. Its net work over a track segment is +16.0 J. Find final speed.',
 ['K_i = ½(2.00)(3.00)² = 9.00 J.',
  'K_f = 9.00 + 16.0 = 25.0 J.',
  'v_f = √(2K_f/m) = √(50.0/2.00) = 5.00 m/s. No elapsed time was needed.'],
 'Use net work, not the work of one selected force unless it is the only force doing work. A speed increase does not identify the direction of the final velocity.',
 [('At fixed mass, how does kinetic energy change if speed triples?', 'K increases by a factor of 3² = 9.'),
  ('A 1.00 kg object at 4.00 m/s receives -6.00 J of net work. Find its final speed.', 'K_i = 8.00 J and K_f = 2.00 J, so v_f = √4.00 = 2.00 m/s.')])
lesson(u, 'Potential energy and conservative forces', [('8-1',213),('7-4',195)],
 'Choose a potential-energy reference and calculate gravitational or spring energy changes.',
 'Potential energy belongs to a system of interacting objects. Near Earth, the object-Earth system has gravitational potential energy U_g = mgh relative to a chosen zero height. The zero is arbitrary; only changes in potential energy influence predictions. A spring stores elastic potential energy ½kx², measured from the relaxed state. Extension and compression of equal magnitude store equal energy.\n\nFor a conservative force, work depends on endpoints rather than the path, and its work is the negative change in potential energy. Gravity does positive work during descent while gravitational potential energy decreases. Stretching a spring requires positive work by the external agent while the spring itself does negative work. Avoid counting both gravitational work and the corresponding potential-energy change twice in the same energy bookkeeping.',
 'U_g = mgh; ΔU_g = mgΔh\nU_s = ½kx²\nW_conservative = -ΔU',
 'Raise a 3.00 kg object by 2.00 m and compress a spring of k = 200 N/m by 0.100 m. Find the stored energy changes.',
 ['ΔU_g = (3.00)(9.81)(2.00) = +58.9 J. The value is independent of where zero height was chosen.',
  'U_s = ½(200)(0.100)² = 1.00 J, using the stated k as the model value.',
  'Gravity does -58.9 J of work during the lift. The spring does -1.00 J of work while being compressed from its relaxed state.'],
 'Use metres for spring compression and height. Potential energy can be negative relative to a chosen reference without implying impossible physics.',
 [('How does spring energy change when compression doubles?', 'It quadruples because U_s is proportional to x².'),
  ('An object drops 5.0 m. Is ΔU_g positive or negative?', 'Negative: ΔU_g = mg(-5.0 m). Gravity’s work is positive.')])
lesson(u, 'Conservation of energy with and without friction', [('8-2',220),('8-4',227),('8-5',231)],
 'Choose a system boundary and include thermal energy when mechanical energy decreases.',
 'Mechanical energy is K + U. It stays constant when the system’s energy changes only through conservative transfers and no energy crosses the boundary. For a falling object-Earth system with negligible drag, gravitational potential energy becomes kinetic energy. This does not mean energy is always only mechanical.\n\nWhen a block slides on a rough track, mechanical energy decreases while thermal energy of the block-track system increases. With that system chosen, use K_i + U_i + W_ext = K_f + U_f + ΔE_th. For simple constant dry friction and a stationary track, ΔE_th ≈ fₖd. If the track is outside the system, account for the energy transfer consistently rather than adding the same friction effect twice. Total energy conservation remains valid even when mechanical energy is not conserved.',
 'frictionless isolated model: K_i + U_i = K_f + U_f\nwith thermal increase: K_i + U_i + W_ext = K_f + U_f + ΔE_th\nΔE_th ≈ fₖd  (simple sliding model)',
 'A 2.00 kg cart starts from rest 3.00 m above the bottom of a track. The cart-track system gains 10.0 J of thermal energy. Find bottom speed.',
 ['Choose the bottom as U_g = 0 and include Earth and the track in the system.',
  'Initial mechanical energy is mgh = 58.86 J. Final kinetic energy is 58.86 - 10.0 = 48.86 J.',
  'v = √[2(48.86)/2.00] = 6.99 m/s. Without the thermal increase the speed would be √(2gh) = 7.67 m/s.'],
 'Friction does not destroy energy. It changes how energy is stored or transferred. State the system before writing a conservation equation.',
 [('A frictionless cart drops from rest through 1.25 m. Find speed.', 'v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.81 × 1.25) = 4.95 m/s.'),
  ('A sliding friction force of 8.0 N acts over 2.5 m on a stationary track. Estimate thermal increase.', 'ΔE_th ≈ fₖd = 20 J for the combined block-track system.')])
lesson(u, 'Power and efficiency', [('7-6',202),('8-5',231)],
 'Distinguish energy from its transfer rate and calculate useful output.',
 'Power is the rate at which energy is transferred or work is done. Two motors can lift the same load through the same height using the same useful energy, while the faster one has higher useful power. Average power uses the energy transferred over an interval. For a force acting parallel to velocity, instantaneous mechanical power is Fv.\n\nEfficiency is useful output energy divided by total input energy, or useful output power divided by input power under corresponding conditions. A real machine often transfers energy into unwanted heating, sound, or deformation. A calculated efficiency above 100% indicates a mismatch of inputs and outputs, measurement problems, or an omitted energy source. A low efficiency does not violate total energy conservation.',
 'P_avg = W/Δt = ΔE/Δt\nP = Fv cos θ\nη = E_useful/E_input × 100% = P_useful/P_input × 100%\n1 W = 1 J/s',
 'A motor lifts 20.0 kg by 5.00 m in 4.00 s while drawing 400 W. Find useful power and efficiency.',
 ['Useful energy is mgh = (20.0)(9.81)(5.00) = 981 J.',
  'Useful average power is 981/4.00 = 245 W.',
  'Input energy is 400 × 4.00 = 1600 J. Efficiency is 981/1600 × 100% = 61.3%, treating 400 W as the assigned input model value.'],
 'Watts are a rate; joules are an amount. Do not divide power by energy to calculate efficiency. Compare like quantities over the same interval.',
 [('A device transfers 600 J in 3.0 s. Find average power.', 'P = 600/3.0 = 200 W.'),
  ('A 75% efficient motor receives 800 J. Find useful output and other energy.', 'Useful output is 600 J; the remaining 200 J transfers into other forms or surroundings.')])
lesson(u, 'Temperature, heat, and specific heat capacity', [('18-1',550),('18-4',558)],
 'Calculate energy transfer for a temperature change without a phase change.',
 'Temperature describes thermal state; it is not the total thermal energy of an object. A large cool object can contain more internal energy than a small hot object. Heat Q names energy transferred because of a temperature difference. Objects have internal energy; “heat” describes a transfer rather than an amount stored inside an object.\n\nSpecific heat capacity c is the energy needed per kilogram per degree of temperature change. Q = mcΔT assumes a suitable approximately constant c and no phase change over the interval. Q is positive for energy absorbed and negative for energy released. A change of 1 °C equals a change of 1 K, even though their absolute temperature scales differ. Use material data supplied by the problem, since values can vary with conditions.',
 'Q = mc(T_f - T_i)\nc unit: J/(kg·K) or J/(kg·°C)\nT_K = T_°C + 273.15\nΔT_K = ΔT_°C',
 'Heat 0.250 kg of water from 20.0 °C to 60.0 °C. Use c = 4180 J/(kg·°C). Find Q.',
 ['The temperature change is 60.0 - 20.0 = +40.0 °C.',
  'Q = (0.250)(4180)(40.0) = 41 800 J = 41.8 kJ.',
  'The positive sign means energy enters the water. Cooling the same mass through the same temperature interval releases the same energy magnitude in this model.'],
 'Do not insert an absolute temperature where the equation needs a temperature difference. Do not use Q = mcΔT across melting or boiling without separately accounting for phase change.',
 [('A 0.500 kg metal sample has c = 900 J/(kg·°C) and cools by 10.0 °C. Find Q.', 'Q = (0.500)(900)(-10.0) = -4500 J using the stated model c.'),
  ('Convert 25.0 °C to kelvin and a 25.0 °C rise to a kelvin rise.', 'Absolute temperature is 298.2 K after rounding to tenths. The temperature rise is 25.0 K.')])
lesson(u, 'Thermal equilibrium and calorimetry', [('18-1',550),('18-4',558),('18-6',570)],
 'Apply energy conservation to objects reaching a shared final temperature.',
 'Objects in thermal contact exchange energy until they reach thermal equilibrium, with a common temperature and no net heat transfer between them. For an insulated mixing system with no work or phase change, the signed heat transfers sum to zero. A warmer object has negative Q and a cooler object positive Q.\n\nInclude a calorimeter, thermometer, or other component when its heat capacity is significant. Heat leaking to the room violates the assumption that the selected system is isolated, not energy conservation itself. Conduction transfers energy through matter without bulk flow, convection involves bulk fluid motion, and radiation can transfer energy across a vacuum. Insulation changes the rate of transfer rather than creating energy.',
 'ΣQ = 0  (insulated, no work or phase change)\nm₁c₁(T_f - T₁) + m₂c₂(T_f - T₂) = 0\nT_f = (m₁c₁T₁ + m₂c₂T₂)/(m₁c₁ + m₂c₂)',
 'Mix 0.200 kg of water at 80.0 °C with 0.300 kg at 20.0 °C in an ideal insulated container. Find equilibrium temperature.',
 ['Both portions have the same c, so it cancels from the conservation equation.',
  '0.200(T_f - 80.0) + 0.300(T_f - 20.0) = 0 gives 0.500T_f = 22.0.',
  'T_f = 44.0 °C. Hot-water transfer is -30 096 J and cool-water transfer is +30 096 J using c = 4180, so the energy balance checks.'],
 'Do not simply average temperatures unless the two heat capacities mc are equal. Use signed changes for every object; do not give both heat transfers positive signs.',
 [('Equal masses of the same material at 10 °C and 50 °C mix ideally. Find T_f.', 'Their heat capacities are equal, so T_f = 30 °C.'),
  ('If a cold metal is put in warm water, must they have equal internal energies at equilibrium?', 'No. They have a common temperature, but internal energies depend on mass, material, and state.')])

u = unit(7, 'Electricity', 'Use charge and energy conservation to solve DC circuits, real batteries, and electrical power problems.',
 'Ch. 24 §24-1; Ch. 26 §§26-1, 26-3 to 26-5; Ch. 27 §§27-1 to 27-3',
 'With a teacher-approved low-voltage battery circuit, measure current through and voltage across a resistor at several settings. Plot V versus I and compare the slope with resistance. Repeat with series and parallel combinations and test junction and loop rules within uncertainty. Use only low-voltage classroom equipment; never investigate a wall outlet. Connect an ammeter in series and a voltmeter across the component.')
lesson(u, 'Charge, current, voltage, and resistance', [('24-1',721),('26-1',781),('26-3',788)],
 'Explain each circuit quantity and connect its definition to its unit.',
 'Current is the rate at which charge passes a cross-section. Conventional current follows the direction a positive charge would move; conduction electrons in a metal drift in the opposite direction. Charge is not used up as it passes through a resistor. In a steady circuit the same current enters and leaves a single unbranched component.\n\nPotential difference, or voltage, is energy change per unit charge between two points. A source transfers energy to charge carriers and components transfer energy to other forms. Resistance relates a component’s voltage to its current at the operating condition. It is measured in ohms. A closed path is needed for sustained current in the simple DC circuit model; an open switch breaks that path.',
 'I = Δq/Δt  (steady current)\nV = ΔE/q  (energy per charge)\nR = V/I\n1 A = 1 C/s; 1 V = 1 J/C; 1 Ω = 1 V/A',
 'A steady current of 0.500 A flows for 12.0 s through a component with a 9.00 V drop. Find charge and transferred energy.',
 ['q = IΔt = (0.500)(12.0) = 6.00 C.',
  'Energy transferred by those charges is E = qV = (6.00)(9.00) = 54.0 J.',
  'The charges continue through the circuit. The component converts or transfers energy; it does not consume the charge.'],
 'Voltage is measured between two points, not “through” a component. Current flows through a component; voltage is across it. Do not confuse charge in coulombs with energy in joules.',
 [('A 2.0 A steady current flows for 5.0 s. How much charge passes?', 'q = It = 10 C.'),
  ('A 12 V source supplies 36 J to charge. How much charge?', 'q = E/V = 36/12 = 3 C.')])
lesson(u, 'Ohm’s law and electrical measurements', [('26-4',792),('27-3',824)],
 'Identify an ohmic component and infer resistance from a graph or circuit measurements.',
 'An ohmic component has approximately constant resistance over the operating range when physical conditions such as temperature stay fixed. Its V-I graph is a straight line through the origin, with slope R when voltage is vertical and current horizontal. Many devices, including filament lamps at changing temperature, are not ohmic over a broad range. The ratio V/I at one point does not prove constant resistance.\n\nAn ammeter measures current and connects in series. Its ideal resistance is zero so that it changes the current as little as possible. A voltmeter measures potential difference and connects in parallel across the component. Its ideal resistance is infinite, drawing no current. Real meters affect the circuit slightly; incorrect ammeter placement across a source can create a short circuit.',
 'V = IR  (constant-R model)\nR = slope of V-versus-I graph\nI = slope of I-versus-V graph × V for a line through origin\nslope of I-versus-V graph = 1/R',
 'An ohmic resistor has measured points (0.100 A, 2.00 V) and (0.300 A, 6.00 V). Find R and current at 9.00 V.',
 ['R = ΔV/ΔI = (6.00 - 2.00)/(0.300 - 0.100) = 20.0 Ω.',
  'Under the same conditions, I = 9.00/20.0 = 0.450 A.',
  'A broad range of measured points would be needed to establish the linear model well. If temperature rises, resistance may change and the prediction may fail.'],
 'Check which quantity is on each graph axis. Do not place an ammeter directly across a battery. Resistance readings should be made on an unpowered, appropriately isolated component using the meter’s instructions.',
 [('A 6.0 V drop gives 0.20 A through an ohmic resistor. Find R.', 'R = 6.0/0.20 = 30 Ω.'),
  ('Why might a lamp’s V-I curve bend?', 'Its filament temperature changes with electrical heating, which changes resistance; the constant-R assumption fails.')])
lesson(u, 'Series circuits', [('27-1',807)],
 'Find equivalent resistance and distribute voltage along one unbranched current path.',
 'Components are in series when they lie on the same unbranched current path. Charge conservation gives the same current through every component. Energy conservation gives a source voltage equal to the sum of component voltage drops in an ideal resistive loop. Adding another series resistor increases total resistance and reduces current for a fixed ideal source.\n\nEquivalent resistance replaces a combination while preserving the source’s voltage-current relationship. For series resistors, R_eq is their sum. First find total current from the source voltage and equivalent resistance, then calculate each drop using its resistance. A larger series resistance takes a larger share of the voltage. One open connection stops current through the entire series path.',
 'R_eq = R₁ + R₂ + ...\nI = V_source/R_eq\nV_i = IR_i\nV_source = V₁ + V₂ + ...',
 'Connect 2.00 Ω and 4.00 Ω in series across an ideal 12.0 V source. Find current and both voltage drops.',
 ['R_eq = 2.00 + 4.00 = 6.00 Ω.',
  'I = 12.0/6.00 = 2.00 A through both resistors.',
  'Drops are (2.00)(2.00) = 4.00 V and (2.00)(4.00) = 8.00 V. Their sum is 12.0 V, satisfying the loop rule.'],
 'Series resistors share current, not necessarily voltage. Geometric placement in a drawing does not define series; trace the nodes and current path.',
 [('Three 10.0 Ω resistors are in series across 15.0 V. Find total current.', 'R_eq = 30.0 Ω and I = 0.500 A.'),
  ('Two series resistors carry 0.40 A. What current enters the second resistor?', '0.40 A; current is not consumed by the first resistor.')])
lesson(u, 'Parallel circuits and mixed networks', [('27-2',817)],
 'Recognize common nodes, find branch currents, and simplify a mixed circuit in stages.',
 'Parallel components connect across the same two nodes, so they have the same potential difference. Current divides among branches and recombines at a junction. A lower-resistance branch carries more current for the same voltage. The equivalent resistance of positive parallel resistors is smaller than the smallest branch resistance.\n\nFor a mixed circuit, identify a true series or parallel group, replace it with its equivalent, and continue until the source current can be found. Then reverse the simplification: recover the voltage across each parallel group and currents through each branch. Components that happen to sit beside each other in a drawing are not necessarily parallel; both endpoint nodes must match.',
 '1/R_eq = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + ...\nfor two branches: R_eq = R₁R₂/(R₁ + R₂)\nI_total = I₁ + I₂ + ...\nI_branch = V_branch/R_branch',
 'A 4.00 Ω resistor is in series with a parallel group of 6.00 Ω and 3.00 Ω across an ideal 12.0 V source.',
 ['The parallel equivalent is (6.00 × 3.00)/(6.00 + 3.00) = 2.00 Ω. Total resistance is 4.00 + 2.00 = 6.00 Ω.',
  'Source current is 2.00 A. The series resistor drops 8.00 V, leaving 4.00 V across the parallel group.',
  'Branch currents are 4.00/6.00 = 0.667 A and 4.00/3.00 = 1.33 A. Their unrounded sum is 2.00 A.'],
 'Do not give a parallel sub-group the full source voltage if another series element takes a voltage drop. Parallel branches share voltage, not current.',
 [('Find the equivalent of two 8.0 Ω resistors in parallel.', 'R_eq = 4.0 Ω.'),
  ('A 6.0 Ω and a 12 Ω resistor are each across 12 V. Find branch and total currents.', 'Branch currents are 2.0 A and 1.0 A; total is 3.0 A. Equivalent resistance is 4.0 Ω.')])
lesson(u, 'Kirchhoff’s junction and loop rules', [('27-1',807),('27-2',817)],
 'Write current and voltage equations with a consistent sign convention.',
 'The junction rule expresses charge conservation in a steady circuit: total current entering a node equals total current leaving it. The loop rule expresses energy conservation per unit charge: the algebraic sum of voltage changes around a closed loop is zero. These rules extend beyond circuits that reduce neatly into series and parallel groups.\n\nChoose branch-current arrows, label every component, and choose a direction for each loop traversal. Across a resistor in the assumed current direction, the voltage change is -IR; going against it gives +IR. Across an ideal source from negative to positive terminal, the change is +ε, and the reverse crossing gives -ε. A negative solved current means the real direction opposes the assumed arrow, not that the solution is invalid.',
 'ΣI_in = ΣI_out\nΣΔV around a closed loop = 0\nresistor along current: ΔV = -IR\nsource from - to +: ΔV = +ε',
 'Two opposing ideal sources of 12.0 V and 4.00 V share a single loop with 2.00 Ω and 6.00 Ω resistors. Find current.',
 ['Assume current in the direction driven by the 12.0 V source.',
  'Loop equation: +12.0 - 4.00 - I(2.00) - I(6.00) = 0.',
  'I = 8.00/8.00 = 1.00 A in the assumed direction. The resistor drops total 8.00 V, balancing the net source rise.'],
 'Do not count every possible loop equation as independent. Use enough independent junction and loop equations for the unknown currents and verify the remaining rules afterward.',
 [('A junction receives 3.0 A and 2.0 A, with one outgoing branch of 1.5 A. Find the other outgoing current.', '3.0 + 2.0 = 1.5 + I gives I = 3.5 A.'),
  ('A solved branch current is -0.20 A. What does that mean?', 'The actual current is 0.20 A opposite the chosen arrow. The algebra has determined its direction.')])
lesson(u, 'EMF, internal resistance, and terminal voltage', [('27-1',807)],
 'Distinguish a battery’s energy-per-charge supply from the voltage delivered under load.',
 'Electromotive force ε is the energy supplied by a source per unit charge. Despite its name, EMF is measured in volts rather than newtons. An ideal battery has no internal resistance. A real battery is modeled as an ideal source in series with an internal resistance r.\n\nWhile the battery discharges, some energy is transferred to thermal energy inside it, so the terminal voltage is ε - Ir. With no current, this model gives terminal voltage equal to EMF. A larger load current creates a larger internal drop. During charging, when current enters the positive terminal, the simple model requires an applied terminal voltage ε + Ir. The discharge expression must not be used with an unexamined current direction.',
 'discharging: V_terminal = ε - Ir\nI = ε/(R_load + r)\nP_internal = I²r\nP_source = εI; P_load = V_terminal I',
 'A battery has ε = 12.0 V and r = 0.500 Ω. Connect a 5.50 Ω load. Find current, terminal voltage, and internal heating power.',
 ['Total resistance is 5.50 + 0.500 = 6.00 Ω, so I = 12.0/6.00 = 2.00 A.',
  'V_terminal = 12.0 - (2.00)(0.500) = 11.0 V. Check: IR_load = (2.00)(5.50) = 11.0 V.',
  'P_internal = (2.00)²(0.500) = 2.00 W. Source power is 24.0 W and load power 22.0 W, balancing the energy rate.'],
 'EMF is not a mechanical force. An unloaded voltage measurement and a loaded voltage measurement need not match. Never deliberately short a battery to test the model.',
 [('A 9.0 V battery with r = 1.0 Ω supplies 0.50 A while discharging. Find terminal voltage.', 'V_terminal = 9.0 - 0.50(1.0) = 8.5 V.'),
  ('Why does terminal voltage drop with a larger discharge current?', 'The internal resistive drop Ir increases; more energy per second is converted to thermal energy inside the source.')])
lesson(u, 'Electrical power, energy, and transmission', [('26-5',796),('27-1',807)],
 'Calculate electrical energy and explain why high-voltage transmission reduces resistive loss.',
 'Electrical power transferred to a component is P = VI. For a resistor obeying V = IR, substitution gives I²R or V²/R. A device using a constant power for time t transfers energy Pt. The kilowatt-hour is an energy unit: one kW operating for one hour transfers 3.6 × 10⁶ J. It is not a power unit.\n\nA transmission line loses power as I²R_line. For a fixed delivered power, increasing transmission voltage allows lower current and therefore much smaller line loss. If voltage is increased by a factor of ten at the same transmitted power, current becomes one tenth and resistive loss becomes one hundredth. This energy argument applies to the resistive line model; actual grid equipment and voltage conversion require additional physics. Circuit breakers and fuses interrupt excessive current to limit overheating.',
 'P = VI\nfor a resistor: P = I²R = V²/R\nE = Pt\n1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J\nP_line loss = I²R_line',
 'A 60.0 W device runs for 2.00 h. Find energy in kWh and joules. A line of total resistance 2.00 Ω carries 10.0 A; find its loss.',
 ['Device energy is (0.0600 kW)(2.00 h) = 0.120 kWh.',
  'In joules, E = (60.0 J/s)(7200 s) = 432 000 J = 432 kJ.',
  'Line loss is (10.0)²(2.00) = 200 W. The total line resistance should include the return path if the circuit has one.'],
 'Use hours with kW for kWh, and seconds with W for joules. Higher voltage does not directly reduce I²R loss unless current is correspondingly reduced for the same power transfer.',
 [('A 12.0 V resistor draws 2.00 A. Find power and energy in 30.0 s.', 'P = 24.0 W; E = 720 J.'),
  ('At fixed transmitted power, voltage doubles. What happens to resistive line loss?', 'Current halves, so I²R loss becomes one quarter if the line resistance is unchanged.')])

u = unit(8, 'Waves & Sound', 'Describe mechanical waves, predict interference and resonance, and connect wave properties to sound.',
 'Ch. 15 §§15-1, 15-6; Ch. 16 §§16-1, 16-2, 16-5, 16-7; Ch. 17',
 'Observe a pulse on a gently stretched rope or use the wave explorer. Track the pulse and one marked point of the medium separately. Then compare standing-wave patterns in an ideal string and an air column. For a low-volume sound investigation, collect resonance or beat data and report room conditions, length measurement, and sound-speed assumptions. Discuss environmental noise and the limits of loudness comparisons without calibrated instruments.')
lesson(u, 'Generating waves: energy and medium motion', [('16-1',480),('17-1',515)],
 'Distinguish transverse and longitudinal waves and separate wave travel from particle motion.',
 'A mechanical wave is a disturbance propagated through an interacting medium. The disturbance carries energy, while particles of the medium oscillate about equilibrium rather than travelling from source to receiver with the wave in the basic model. A rope marker moves up and down while the pulse travels along the rope. Sound in air involves back-and-forth particle motion and alternating compressions and rarefactions.\n\nA transverse wave has particle motion perpendicular to propagation; a longitudinal wave has particle motion parallel to propagation. A single pulse differs from a repeating periodic wave. Straight wavefronts describe a locally plane wave; circular wavefronts spread from a source in a two-dimensional medium. Rays indicate propagation direction and are perpendicular to wavefronts in a uniform isotropic medium.',
 'transverse: particle motion ⟂ propagation\nlongitudinal: particle motion ∥ propagation\nwavefront = points at the same phase',
 'A pulse travels 3.0 m along a rope in 0.50 s. A marked point rises 0.040 m and returns. Compare wave speed and marker displacement.',
 ['Wave speed is 3.0/0.50 = 6.0 m/s along the rope.',
  'The marker’s motion is transverse, not 3.0 m along the rope. Its final displacement is zero after the pulse passes in the simple model.',
  'The wave carried energy through the rope even though the marker returned to its original position.'],
 'A wave’s speed is not the same as the instantaneous speed of one particle of the medium. Water surface waves involve more complex particle motion than a perfectly transverse rope model.',
 [('What makes sound in air longitudinal?', 'Air particles oscillate parallel to the direction in which compressions and rarefactions travel.'),
  ('Can mechanical sound travel through an ideal vacuum?', 'No. Mechanical sound requires a material medium with particles that interact.')])
lesson(u, 'Amplitude, wavelength, frequency, period, and speed', [('15-1',449),('16-1',480),('16-2',488)],
 'Read wave properties from spatial and time graphs and use v = fλ.',
 'Amplitude A is the maximum displacement from equilibrium. Wavelength λ is the distance between neighboring points in the same phase, such as crest to crest in a sinusoidal snapshot. Period T is the time for one oscillation at a fixed location, and frequency f counts oscillations per second. One hertz means one cycle per second.\n\nIn one period a travelling periodic pattern advances one wavelength, giving v = λ/T = fλ. The source sets frequency while the medium and its conditions determine wave speed. A displacement-position graph shows wavelength; a displacement-time graph at one point shows period. A change in amplitude alone does not change speed in the linear ideal model. For a stretched string, speed increases with tension and decreases with linear mass density.',
 'f = 1/T\nv = fλ = λ/T\nstring wave speed = √(F_tension/μ_linear)\nμ_linear = string mass/string length',
 'A wave has wavelength 0.80 m and frequency 5.0 Hz. Find period and speed. It enters a medium where its speed is 2.0 m/s.',
 ['T = 1/5.0 = 0.20 s.',
  'v = (5.0)(0.80) = 4.0 m/s in the first medium.',
  'At a stationary boundary the transmitted frequency stays 5.0 Hz, so the new wavelength is 2.0/5.0 = 0.40 m.'],
 'A crest-to-trough vertical separation is 2A, not A. A crest-to-next-trough horizontal separation is λ/2, not λ.',
 [('A wave period is 0.025 s and speed is 12 m/s. Find f and λ.', 'f = 40 Hz; λ = v/f = 0.30 m.'),
  ('String tension increases by a factor of four with unchanged linear density. How does wave speed change?', 'Speed doubles because v is proportional to the square root of tension.')])
lesson(u, 'Reflection, refraction, transmission, and diffraction', [('16-7',501),('17-1',515)],
 'Predict wave behavior at a boundary or opening and state which quantities change.',
 'At a boundary, part of a wave can reflect and part can transmit. A transverse pulse reflected from a fixed end inverts; at a free end it reflects without inversion. Boundary behavior depends on how the medium is constrained. For sound, distinguish displacement patterns from pressure patterns instead of transferring rope rules blindly.\n\nRefraction changes propagation direction when wave speed changes across a boundary and incidence is not normal. Frequency stays fixed by the source at a stationary boundary, so wavelength changes with speed. Diffraction is spreading around obstacles or through openings, and is more pronounced when an opening or obstacle size is comparable with wavelength. These boundary ideas extend the source’s wave and interference treatment to the curriculum’s qualitative wave behaviors.',
 'at a stationary boundary: f₁ = f₂\nλ₂/λ₁ = v₂/v₁\nrefraction: sin θ₁/sin θ₂ = v₁/v₂\nangles measured from the normal',
 'A wave enters a slower medium at an angle. Its speed drops from 4.0 to 2.0 m/s while frequency remains 5.0 Hz. Explain wavelength and direction.',
 ['The original wavelength is 4.0/5.0 = 0.80 m and the transmitted wavelength is 2.0/5.0 = 0.40 m.',
  'The refraction relation gives a smaller angle from the normal in the slower medium, so the ray bends toward the normal.',
  'For normal incidence there is no bending, although wavelength still changes. Boundary conditions may also produce a reflected wave.'],
 'Refraction angles are measured from the normal, not the surface. “Frequency stays the same” assumes a stationary boundary and the same driving source.',
 [('What happens to a rope pulse reflected at a fixed end?', 'Its transverse displacement inverts: an upward incident pulse reflects as a downward pulse.'),
  ('Which opening produces stronger spreading for the same wave: much wider than λ or comparable to λ?', 'An opening comparable to λ generally produces more pronounced diffraction.')])
lesson(u, 'Superposition and interference', [('16-5',494),('17-3',521)],
 'Add simultaneous displacements and relate path difference to reinforcement or cancellation.',
 'In a linear medium, superposition says the resultant displacement at a point is the algebraic sum of the individual wave displacements. Crest meeting crest produces constructive interference; crest meeting trough can produce destructive interference. Equal opposite displacements cancel at that instant, but the waves can continue beyond each other. Local cancellation does not mean the whole system’s energy vanishes.\n\nFor two coherent sources initially in phase, a path difference of an integer number of wavelengths gives constructive interference, and a half-integer gives destructive interference. The phase relationship matters: initially out-of-phase sources reverse those conditions. Stable interference requires a consistent relative phase; unrelated changing sources do not make a fixed pattern.',
 'y_resultant = y₁ + y₂\nin-phase sources: constructive Δr = nλ\nin-phase sources: destructive Δr = (n + ½)λ\nn = 0, 1, 2, ...',
 'At a point, two pulses have displacements +3.0 cm and -1.0 cm. Separately, two in-phase sources have λ = 0.40 m and path difference 0.60 m.',
 ['Pulse displacement adds algebraically: +3.0 + (-1.0) = +2.0 cm.',
  'The path difference ratio is 0.60/0.40 = 1.5 wavelengths.',
  'This is a half-integer, so the coherent in-phase waves interfere destructively there. Complete cancellation also requires equal amplitudes.'],
 'Interference depends on phase, not just whether sources are nearby. “Destructive” does not guarantee zero amplitude when the interfering amplitudes differ.',
 [('Two identical +2.0 cm pulses overlap completely. Find resultant displacement.', 'y = +4.0 cm during the overlap in the linear model.'),
  ('In-phase sources have path difference 2λ. Is interference constructive?', 'Yes. The arrival phases differ by two complete cycles.')])
lesson(u, 'Standing waves, resonance, and string harmonics', [('15-6',468),('16-7',501)],
 'Locate nodes and antinodes and determine the allowed frequencies of a fixed-end string.',
 'A standing wave forms from interference of travelling waves moving in opposite directions. Nodes remain at zero displacement; antinodes have the largest oscillation amplitude. Neighboring nodes are separated by λ/2, and a node and its neighboring antinode by λ/4. The pattern appears stationary even though the medium oscillates between nodes.\n\nA string fixed at both ends must have displacement nodes at both ends. Its length contains n half-wavelengths, so λ_n = 2L/n and f_n = nv/(2L). The lowest frequency is the fundamental; higher integer multiples are harmonics. Resonance occurs when a periodic drive matches a natural frequency, allowing substantial energy transfer and a large response. Damping limits the amplitude of real resonance.',
 'fixed ends: L = nλ_n/2\nλ_n = 2L/n\nf_n = nv/(2L), n = 1, 2, 3, ...\nnode spacing = λ/2',
 'A string is 0.800 m long and wave speed is 120 m/s. Find the first three harmonic frequencies and the second-harmonic wavelength.',
 ['Fundamental f₁ = 120/[2(0.800)] = 75.0 Hz, using the assigned speed value.',
  'f₂ = 150 Hz and f₃ = 225 Hz. The corresponding patterns have two and three half-wavelength segments.',
  'λ₂ = 2(0.800)/2 = 0.800 m. The second harmonic has a node at the middle as well as at both fixed ends.'],
 'A standing wave’s antinode is a location, not a crest permanently frozen in place. For equal counter-propagating waves the ideal standing pattern has no net average energy flow along the string.',
 [('A fixed-end string has f₁ = 60 Hz. Find f₄.', 'f₄ = 4f₁ = 240 Hz.'),
  ('Nodes are 0.25 m apart. Find wavelength.', 'Neighboring node separation is λ/2, so λ = 0.50 m.')])
lesson(u, 'Sound speed, pitch, intensity, and sound level', [('17-1',515),('17-4',524)],
 'Connect frequency to pitch, intensity to sound level, and travel time to distance.',
 'Sound in air is a longitudinal mechanical wave. Use 343 m/s for air at about 20 °C when assigned; temperature and the medium affect the speed. Pitch is strongly associated with frequency. Loudness is a perceptual response influenced by intensity, frequency, and the listener, so it is not identical to amplitude or a decibel number.\n\nIntensity is power passing through unit area. An ideal point source spreading uniformly in three dimensions gives I = P/(4πr²). Doubling distance then reduces intensity to one quarter. Sound level is logarithmic: a tenfold intensity increase adds 10 dB, while doubling intensity adds about 3 dB. For a fixed medium and frequency in the linear model, intensity scales with squared displacement amplitude.',
 'sound speed in air at 20 °C: v ≈ 343 m/s\nI = P/A; point source I = P/(4πr²)\nβ = 10 log₁₀(I/I₀) dB\nI₀ = 1.0 × 10⁻¹² W/m²\necho distance = vt_round-trip/2',
 'An echo returns after 0.800 s in air at 20 °C. Find reflecting-wall distance. A sound has intensity 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ W/m²; find sound level.',
 ['The sound travels to the wall and back, so d = (343)(0.800)/2 = 137 m.',
  'I/I₀ = 10⁶, so β = 10 log₁₀(10⁶) = 60 dB.',
  'A tenfold intensity increase would make 70 dB; it would not multiply the decibel reading by ten.'],
 'Do not omit the factor of two for an echo. A higher amplitude changes intensity rather than frequency in the ideal model. A given dB increase is not a universal loudness multiplier.',
 [('An ideal point-source intensity is measured at r and 2r. Compare the values.', 'I(2r) = I(r)/4, assuming the same source and no additional absorption or reflection.'),
  ('Intensity increases from I to 100I. How much does β rise?', 'Δβ = 10 log₁₀(100) = 20 dB.')])
lesson(u, 'Air-column resonance and beats', [('17-5',528),('17-6',532)],
 'Distinguish open and closed pipe harmonics and calculate beat frequency.',
 'For air-particle displacement, an open end is approximately an antinode and a closed rigid end a node. Pressure nodes and antinodes are reversed. An open-open pipe supports integer harmonics with f_n = nv/(2L). A pipe closed at one end and open at the other fits odd quarter-wavelength patterns, so f_n = nv/(4L) for n = 1, 3, 5, and so on. These simple formulas neglect end corrections.\n\nTwo nearby frequencies interfere to produce beats: a slow variation of amplitude with frequency |f₁ - f₂|. Beats help compare or tune sound sources. The beat frequency gives the magnitude of their frequency difference but alone does not show which source is higher. Resonance and beats are different phenomena: resonance involves a driven natural mode, while beats arise from overlapping nearby frequencies.',
 'open-open pipe: f_n = nv/(2L), n = 1, 2, 3, ...\nopen-closed pipe: f_n = nv/(4L), n = 1, 3, 5, ...\nf_beat = |f₁ - f₂|',
 'An open-closed pipe has L = 0.500 m in air with v = 343 m/s. Find its first two allowed frequencies. Compare 440 Hz and 444 Hz tones.',
 ['Fundamental f₁ = 343/[4(0.500)] = 171.5 Hz ≈ 172 Hz.',
  'The next allowed frequency is f₃ = 3f₁ = 514.5 Hz ≈ 515 Hz. The n = 2 harmonic is absent in this ideal pipe.',
  'The two tones produce |444 - 440| = 4 Hz beats, meaning four amplitude cycles per second.'],
 'For an open-closed pipe, the second allowed mode is the third harmonic. Say whether a node refers to particle displacement or pressure.',
 [('An open-open pipe has fundamental 200 Hz. Find its next two harmonic frequencies.', '400 Hz and 600 Hz.'),
  ('A 256 Hz fork beats at 3 Hz against another fork. What frequencies might the other fork have?', '253 Hz or 259 Hz. More evidence is needed to decide which.')])
lesson(u, 'Doppler shifts and sonic booms', [('17-7',534),('17-8',539)],
 'Predict frequency changes from relative motion and distinguish a sonic boom from ordinary Doppler shift.',
 'A moving observer encounters wavefronts more or less frequently. A moving source changes the spacing of emitted wavefronts in the medium. Approach increases the observed frequency; recession decreases it. These shifts change the heard pitch without changing the source’s own emitted frequency. Sound-speed formulas use motion relative to the medium, not just an unspecified relative speed.\n\nFor a stationary observer and a source approaching at speed v_s less than sound speed v, f_observed = fv/(v - v_s). A source moving away uses v + v_s in the denominator. When a source moves faster than sound, successive disturbances form a shock front and a receiver can hear a sonic boom as that front passes. The boom is not a single event produced only at the instant the source first crosses the sound speed.',
 'source approaches stationary observer: f_obs = f v/(v - v_s)\nsource recedes: f_obs = f v/(v + v_s)\nobserver approaches stationary source: f_obs = f(v + v_o)/v\nMach number = source speed/sound speed',
 'A 500 Hz siren moves toward a stationary listener at 20.0 m/s in still air. Use v = 343 m/s.',
 ['The source approaches, so emitted wavefronts ahead are closer together and the heard frequency increases.',
  'f_obs = 500 × 343/(343 - 20.0) ≈ 531 Hz, treating 500 Hz as the assigned source value.',
  'If the source instead recedes at the same speed, f_obs = 500 × 343/363 ≈ 472 Hz. The source still emits 500 Hz in its own description.'],
 'Source-motion and observer-motion formulas differ. State whether the air is still and whether the specified speed is relative to the medium. The elementary subsonic formula is not a sonic-boom model.',
 [('A listener approaches a stationary 600 Hz source at 10.0 m/s in still air. Find observed frequency using v = 343 m/s.', 'f_obs = 600(343 + 10.0)/343 ≈ 617 Hz using the assigned source frequency.'),
  ('A source moves at 686 m/s where sound speed is 343 m/s. Find Mach number.', 'Mach number is 686/343 = 2.00, so the source is supersonic.')])
